{"name":"Deep Blue","hex":"#040273","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep blue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["An intense, relatively dark blue.","Of a geographic area, state, or district, heavily and consistently voting for the Democratic Party."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An intense, relative dark blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep_blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Deep Blue (musician)","blocks":[{"text":"Deep Blue is the alias of electronic and drum and bass musician Sean O'Keeffe."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Blue_(musician)"},{"title":"Deep Blue (chess computer)","blocks":[{"text":"Deep Blue was a customized IBM RS/6000 SP supercomputer for chess-playing designed by computer scientist Feng-hsiung Hsu. It was the first computer to win a game, and the first to win a match, against a reigning world champion under regular time controls. Development began in 1985 at Carnegie Mellon University under the name ChipTest. It then moved to IBM, where it was first renamed Deep Thought, then again in 1989 to Deep Blue. In 1996, it was used to compete against world champion Garry Kasparov in a six-game match, where it won one, drew two, and lost three games. In 1997, it underwent an upgrade, and in a six-game rematch it defeated Kasparov by winning two games and drawing three. Deep Blue's victory is considered a milestone in the history of artificial intelligence and has been the subject of several books and films."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Deep_Blue.jpg/330px-Deep_Blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Blue_(chess_computer)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Kasparov_Magath_1985_Hamburg-2.png/500px-Kasparov_Magath_1985_Hamburg-2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Kasparov_Magath_1985_Hamburg-2.png/500px-Kasparov_Magath_1985_Hamburg-2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Garry Kasparov playing a simultaneous exhibition in 1985","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKasparov_Magath_1985_Hamburg-2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/One_of_Deep_Blue%27s_processors_%282586060990%29.jpg/500px-One_of_Deep_Blue%27s_processors_%282586060990%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/One_of_Deep_Blue%27s_processors_%282586060990%29.jpg/1280px-One_of_Deep_Blue%27s_processors_%282586060990%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/One_of_Deep_Blue%27s_processors_%282586060990%29.jpg/500px-One_of_Deep_Blue%27s_processors_%282586060990%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of Deep 